Vulvodynia
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Chronic unprovoked vulvar burning, Severe entry dyspareunia, Allodynia on cotton swab test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-050
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe psychological crisis and uncontrollable sobbing triggered by another failed, agonizing attempt at intercourse.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute psychiatric decompensation, severe hyperventilation, and acute, burning vulvar pain following an attempt at intimacy. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to de-escalate the acute panic attack, provide immediate targeted topical analgesia for the provoked physical pain, and address the severe distress regarding the impact on her intimate relationship before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The burning is so agonizing I cannot bear to be touched, and I am terrified this is going to completely destroy my relationship.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a continuous, 8-month history of unprovoked burning, stinging, and raw pain of the vulva, persisting without identifiable cause. Tonight, the crisis was triggered by severe entry dyspareunia making sexual intercourse exceedingly painful and ultimately impossible. She complains of chronic hyperesthesia and allodynia (severe pain from light touch) at the vulvar vestibule. The pain is exacerbated daily by wearing tight clothing or prolonged sitting. She reports a history of negative cultures for bacterial or fungal pathogens. Tonight, she is experiencing significant psychological distress and a severe panic attack regarding the impact on her intimate relationship.
Functional Status: Pacing, highly agitated, crying uncontrollably.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe psychological distress and relationship strain; partner is supportive but feels helpless.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/85 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient is acutely distressed, hyperventilating, and tearful.
Physical Exam: Visual inspection of the vulva reveals a complete absence of gross observable inflammatory, neoplastic, or infectious lesions. No erythema, edema, or characteristic discharge is present. A standardized cotton swab test (gently touching the vulvar vestibule with a Q-tip) demonstrates severe, localized allodynia (the patient flinches violently and reports 9/10 burning pain from the light touch). Vaginal pH testing is normal (4.0).
Environmental Observations: Clean environment; partner is present and highly distressed by the patient's pain.
Medication Reconciliation: Has tried numerous over-the-counter thrush treatments with zero efficacy and worsening burning.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Provoked Vestibulodynia (Vulvodynia) with secondary acute stress/panic reaction - Status: Acute physical and psychological flare
Differentials:

Chronic Recurrent Candidiasis - Definitively ruled out by the normal vaginal pH, absence of curdy discharge, negative historical cultures, and complete lack of clinical signs of fungal inflammation.

Genital Herpes (HSV) - Ruled out by the chronic 8-month continuous nature of the pain and the complete absence of vesicles or ulcerations on examination.

Atrophic Vaginitis - Unlikely given the patient's age (pre-menopausal) and the visually normal, non-thinned, non-pale mucosa.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to psychological crisis)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is presenting with an acute psychological crisis triggered by the severe, chronic physical reality of provoked vulvodynia. The objective clinical examination confirms the diagnostic tetrad: chronic burning, severe entry dyspareunia, and profound allodynia on a cotton swab test, in the complete absence of observable skin disease or infection. The acute panic, hyperventilation, and relationship distress required immediate after-hours intervention to provide robust clinical validation of this specific neurological pain syndrome, administer acute local anesthetic to break the physical pain cycle, and de-escalate the psychiatric crisis. Delaying assessment would have resulted in an unnecessary emergency department presentation for a psychiatric crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted 10 minutes of guided diaphragmatic breathing to successfully resolve the hyperventilation and tachycardia. Applied a compound 5% lidocaine ointment directly to the vulvar vestibule, resulting in immediate, profound relief of the burning allodynia. Provided acute psychological reassurance to both the patient and partner, explicitly validating that the pain is a real, measurable neurological condition, not an infection or a psychological manifestation. Prescribed oral amitriptyline 10mg strictly at night to begin downregulating the overactive nerve pathways.
Positive Behavioral Change: To instantly stop the pain signal before attempting any intimate touch in the future, apply the prescribed lidocaine ointment exactly 15 minutes beforehand; experiencing the profound numbness will rebuild your confidence and reduce the anticipatory anxiety. To begin retraining the overactive nerves, practice daily pelvic floor relaxation (reverse Kegel) exercises for 5 minutes; consciously feeling the muscles drop and release will slowly break the chronic tension loop causing the pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized pelvic floor physiotherapist and a pain-psychologist.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in two weeks to assess the efficacy of the amitriptyline and arrange a referral to a dedicated vulvar pain clinic.

