PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a complex chronic pain condition; the medication tonight is the start of a long-term neurological retraining process.
Clinical Course: The numbing ointment will provide relief for several hours tonight. The low-dose antidepressant (amitriptyline) takes several weeks to fully calm the nerve endings in the pelvis.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you feel you cannot keep yourself safe, or if you develop thoughts of self-harm.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You do not have an infection, and this is not in your head. You have Vulvodynia, which means the nerve endings at the entrance to your vagina have become hypersensitive and are misfiring, interpreting light touch as severe burning pain. The numbing ointment physically turns off those nerve endings temporarily. The tablet I prescribed is a very low-dose nerve medication that will slowly retrain those nerves to stop firing inappropriately over the next few months.
Actionable Steps: Take the amitriptyline 10mg tonight before bed. Stop using all over-the-counter thrush treatments immediately, as they irritate the nerves further. Make an appointment with your GP to get a referral to a specialist pelvic pain clinic.

